  "marked": false,
  "answer": "<p>To ensure that your custom Chef recipes automatically run when new instances are launched with AWS OpsWorks, you need to both <b>enable custom cookbooks support at the stack and layer levels</b> within OpsWorks. This will make your recipes available and executable as needed on each instance.<br><br>The option <b>\"Verify that the custom cookbooks feature is enabled in the OpsWorks layer settings.\"</b> is required—layers are where you configure which recipes actually execute during various lifecycle events for the instances in that layer. Enabling custom cookbooks at the layer level allows those custom cookbooks to be associated directly with the instances.<br><br>The option <b>\"Confirm the custom cookbooks option is selected at the OpsWorks stack level.\"</b> is also necessary, as the stack needs to know about custom cookbook locations and general usage. If it is not enabled at the stack level, you cannot use them in layer configuration.<br><br>The other options are not fully correct: <i>\"Make sure the recipe is added under the Setup event in the Stack lifecycle event configuration.\"</i> is incorrect because lifecycle events (like Setup) are managed at the layer, not the stack, level. <i>\"Add the recipe to the Setup lifecycle event of the layer configuration.\"</i> alone is not sufficient unless custom cookbooks are enabled; prior configuration is mandatory.<br><br><b>Tip:</b> Always enable and configure custom cookbooks first at the stack level, then reference them as needed in the layer configuration.</p>",
  "query": "<p>As a DevOps Engineer, you are setting up an OpsWorks stack on AWS for your company. There is a requirement to run specific custom Chef recipes—already used in your on-premise setup—every time an instance launches within OpsWorks.<br>Which two actions should you perform to achieve this requirement?</p>",
